Performance Baselines and the Standard Deviation

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.933
Welcome back to our session on quality baseline.

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:12.033
Where we had previously looked at variation

4
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.366
in a set of data, like measurement of the length of these lines.

5
00:00:15.366 --> 00:00:19.432
To compute a standard deviation, which is kind of like an inch or a pound.

6
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:23.533
It tells us the standard amount by which a measurement in here

7
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:26.466
would deviate from its expected value.

8
00:00:26.466 --> 00:00:30.066
Of course we're not going to get into the specifics of that right now.

9
00:00:30.066 --> 00:00:34.666
Then we took that example we went over and  looked at IQ.

10
00:00:34.666 --> 00:00:39.332
Ultimately to a process forming a histogram.

11
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:46.299
Now, what I want to do is come back to this notion of a normal distribution.

12
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.666
So, here's some performance characteristic that we'll call Y.

13
00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:56.132
And its balance point right here, is the average.

14
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:00.399
I’m just going to let that be AVG for average for right now

15
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.400
because this is such a cursory overview of what we’re doing.

16
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.766
I’m now go to pick a point out here on Y.

17
00:01:08.766 --> 00:01:12.766
Let's say that this is IQ. Remember, that it was 100.

18
00:01:12.766 --> 00:01:19.766
And remember, over here I’m going to pick a point. Now, I’m going to say an IQ of 120.

19
00:01:19.766 --> 00:01:26.199
Now by taking all the IQ measurements in computing the standard deviation,

20
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.200
which we designate by the symbol σ.

21
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.733
Let's recognize that σ is a function of variation

22
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:38.866
that is out there.

23
00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:42.066
I’m not going to get into the specifics of how to compute the standard deviation.

24
00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:45.899
Let's just represent, that it's a computed number

25
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.166
and let's suppose that we compute it to be 10.

26
00:01:51.166 --> 00:01:58.132
What this would say is that the distance between 100 and 120.

27
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:04.466
That distance. 10 and 10.

28
00:02:04.466 --> 00:02:11.232
Or 120-100 is 20.

29
00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:17.966
So, there's 10, 20. That is 2 σ.

30
00:02:17.966 --> 00:02:24.932
Two standard deviations. So, we compute for the range of variation that we see.

31
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:31.233
The standard deviation. For the range of variation. In this case, it was 10.

32
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:38.199
And now we discover, given the magnitude of that. That there are 2

33
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.200
standard deviations from 100 to 120.

34
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:48.033
Now, statisticians have already made the association

35
00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:51.833
between numbers of standard deviations

36
00:02:51.833 --> 00:02:58.599
and how much area is under this curve, beyond that point we're interested in.

37
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.866
So, what we want to do is to consult what's called a table

38
00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:11.366
of area under the normal curve. A table of area under the normal curve.

39
00:03:11.366 --> 00:03:15.099
And it will tell us how much area

40
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.800
is beyond 120.

41
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.633
That's the probability of getting a person with an IQ

42
00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:32.299
greater than 120. By the area under the curve, because we know 100%

43
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:38.066
of the area under this curve is between a minimum IQ

44
00:03:38.066 --> 00:03:42.966
and out here near infinite IQ. So 100% of IQ’s

45
00:03:42.966 --> 00:03:48.632
fit under the area of the normal curve. So, if I look at the average.

46
00:03:48.633 --> 00:03:55.066
And I happen to know that 50%

47
00:03:55.066 --> 00:04:00.366
of the total area under this curve is between the average and here. 50% is over here.

48
00:04:00.366 --> 00:04:06.766
So, if I know this amount of area,

49
00:04:06.766 --> 00:04:10.766
then by subtraction I can find this tail area.

50
00:04:10.766 --> 00:04:14.799
And that's what a table of area under the normal curve helps us do.

51
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.800
Now let's just suppose, for the sake of argument,

52
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:25.266
that we define that tale area as a probability or as p.

53
00:04:25.266 --> 00:04:31.332
Thus, we would know, that for some σ,

54
00:04:31.333 --> 00:04:36.933
Kσ is equivalent to a certain probability.

55
00:04:36.933 --> 00:04:41.166
We will come back and revisit this idea.

56
00:04:41.166 --> 00:04:46.166
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
